{"data":{"id":"10.5281/zenodo.20622878","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.5281/zenodo.20622878","prefix":"10.5281","suffix":"zenodo.20622878","identifiers":[],"alternateIdentifiers":[],"creators":[{"name":"Dietz, Joel","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joel","familyName":"Dietz","nameIdentifiers":[{"nameIdentifier":"0009-0007-4948-9192","nameIdentifierScheme":"ORCID"}],"affiliation":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NeuroEval: Evaluating Data Transparency, Reproducibility, and Neural-Coding Rigor in Computational and Biohybrid/Wetware Neuroscience Papers"}],"publisher":"Zenodo","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"computational neuroscience"},{"subject":"biohybrid"},{"subject":"wetware"},{"subject":"organoid"},{"subject":"DishBrain"},{"subject":"reproducibility"},{"subject":"neural coding"},{"subject":"information theory"},{"subject":"neural simulators"},{"subject":"NeuroML"},{"subject":"spiking neural networks"},{"subject":"model resolvability"},{"subject":"research software evaluation"},{"subject":"FAIR data"},{"subject":"large language models"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-10-26","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":"en","types":{"ris":"GEN","bibtex":"misc","citeproc":"article","schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceType":"","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsSupplementTo","relatedIdentifier":"https://github.com/fractastical/bioinformatics-eval","relatedIdentifierType":"URL"},{"relationType":"HasVersion","relatedIdentifier":"10.5281/zenodo.20690622","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"},{"relationType":"HasVersion","relatedIdentifier":"10.5281/zenodo.20622879","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"},{"relationType":"HasVersion","relatedIdentifier":"10.5281/zenodo.20647608","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"},{"relationType":"HasVersion","relatedIdentifier":"10.5281/zenodo.20636323","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"},{"relationType":"HasVersion","relatedIdentifier":"10.5281/zenodo.20787777","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"},{"relationType":"HasVersion","relatedIdentifier":"10.5281/zenodo.23656098","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1.0.2","rightsList":[{"rights":"MIT License","rightsUri":"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rightsIdentifier":"mit","rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX"}],"descriptions":[{"description":"Report edition 1.0.2, evaluated under unchanged rubric 1.0.1. This full-corpus edition incorporates only the Arbor and BindsNET maintainer-feedback reassessments; all other scores are retained without rerunning. The corrected entries use current commit-pinned software evidence plus the original paper, with Sonnet scoring, Opus editorial reassessment and human corrections. No benchmarks or CI were independently executed. Edition and rubric versions are intentionally separate.\n\nThis deposition bundles the NeuroEval Reproducibility Report — an arXiv-style preprint that explains how the NeuroEval evaluation system works and presents its evaluations of a corpus of computational and biohybrid/wetware neuroscience papers — together with the complete, MIT-licensed source code of the system. Two files are included: the report PDF and a full archive of the source at the corresponding revision.\n\nNeuroscience papers increasingly rest either on neural simulators — NEURON and NetPyNE, NEST, Brian2, The Virtual Brain, Nengo, PyNN, and spiking-network libraries — or on biohybrid/wetware platforms (MEA cultures, organoids, and closed-loop DishBrain/CL1 systems), yet the reproducibility of the models and systems they describe is rarely assessed systematically. NeuroEval is an LLM-driven framework that submits a paper by URL or PDF, extracts its real text (and errors out rather than scoring unextractable input), gathers external evidence, and scores the paper on a versioned seven-dimension rubric using Anthropic Claude.\n\nSix dimensions measure conformance to good data and reproducibility practice and together make up the Reproducibility score: Data \u0026 Stimulus Disclosure (15%), Dataset/Model Resolvability (12%), Simulator \u0026 Code Availability (12%), Model-to-Code Traceability (15%), Neural Model Spec Clarity (14%), and Reproducibility Package Quality (7%). Identifiers are resolved live against the neuroscience standards ecosystem — ModelDB, NeuroML-DB, Open Source Brain, the NeuroML/SONATA/PyNN toolchains, DANDI, and BioModels, plus repository facts and a Crossref/DataCite DOI resolver — rather than trusting the paper's own claims.\n\nA seventh, orthogonal dimension, Neural Information Processing / Representation-Geometry Rigor (25%), scores whether a paper formalizes and quantifies how the system encodes or transforms information. As of rubric 0.2.0 this admits both classical information theory (spike-train entropy, mutual and transfer information, channel capacity, bits per spike, coding efficiency) and population/representation geometry (Fisher information, representational dissimilarity, decoder discriminability, neural-manifold dimensionality and capacity) — but geometry counts only when it is tied to discriminability, decoding, or behavior; a bare PCA/UMAP/t-SNE projection earns no credit. It measures scientific-content rigor, not transparency; infrastructure and simulator-tool papers that make no information-processing claim are scored at a neutral baseline (~50 on the 0–100 scale), while simulator-support work can exceed the midpoint when it enables reproducible representation or information analysis. It carries the single largest weight of the seven dimensions. The overall score is the weighted mean of the seven dimensions.\n\nThe bundled report applies the rubric (version 1.0.1) to a corpus of computational and biohybrid/wetware neuroscience projects, reporting results as a split: a Reproducibility score (the six transparency dimensions, renormalized to 100), the orthogonal information-processing axis on its own, and the combined seven-dimension Overall. Its headline finding is that transparency is uneven and reproducibility packaging consistently weak, while quantified neural-information-processing rigor remains the exception even across projects whose modeled phenomenon is information processing itself, with infrastructure and tool papers correctly resting at the neutral baseline.\n\nThe scoring rubric remains version 1.0.1. NeuroEval shares its evaluation engine with its sibling tool BioEval but carries a neuroscience rubric and registry spanning both simulation and biohybrid/wetware work. Built as a pnpm monorepo: React + Vite frontend, Express 5 API, PostgreSQL + Drizzle ORM, contract-first OpenAPI/Orval codegen.","descriptionType":"Abstract"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"xml":"","url":"https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.20622878","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":6,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"api","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"viewsOverTime":[],"downloadCount":0,"downloadsOverTime":[],"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"citationsOverTime":[{"year":"2026","total":1}],"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":7,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-10T08:03:18.000Z","registered":"2026-06-10T08:03:18.000Z","published":"2026","updated":"2026-10-26T14:16:57.000Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"cern.zenodo","type":"clients"}},"provider":{"data":{"id":"cern","type":"providers"}},"media":{"data":{"id":"10.5281/zenodo.20622878","type":"media"}},"references":{"data":[]},"citations":{"data":[]},"parts":{"data":[]},"partOf":{"data":[]},"versions":{"data":[{"id":"10.5281/zenodo.20622879","type":"dois"},{"id":"10.5281/zenodo.20636323","type":"dois"},{"id":"10.5281/zenodo.23656098","type":"dois"},{"id":"10.5281/zenodo.20690622","type":"dois"},{"id":"10.5281/zenodo.20787777","type":"dois"},{"id":"10.5281/zenodo.20647608","type":"dois"},{"id":"10.5281/zenodo.20622878","type":"dois"}]},"versionOf":{"data":[{"id":